-- Purpose: replace the two stored events whose geometry arrived from EONET with
--          latitude and longitude reversed.
-- Milestone: task 3.1 of fix-eonet-polygon-transposition.
-- Data quality: production-quality. Operates on real ingested data.
--
-- Why this exists
-- ---------------
-- EONET republishes GDACS polygons with the coordinate pair reversed. The
-- resolver added alongside this migration corrects NEW ingestions, but never
-- rewrites rows already stored -- so without this, production keeps serving a
-- flood in Kwara State, Nigeria that never happened. The event is real; it
-- occurred in Cameroon.
--
-- ⚠️ EPISODE MATCHING IS LOAD-BEARING, and an earlier revision of this migration
-- got it wrong. GDACS events move: 1104105 ran 13 Aug to 4 Sep over five
-- episodes in different parts of Nigeria. EONET publishes ONE of them. Taking
-- the event-level centroid -- which is what the event endpoint returns, and what
-- the first revision hardcoded -- would have relocated that flood ~700km into
-- the Niger Delta rather than correcting it. Each polygon below is the GDACS
-- episode whose vertex count AND extent match what EONET published.
--
-- ⚠️ The POLYGON is restored, not a centroid. GetNearbyEvents runs ST_DWithin
-- against geom, which for a polygon measures to the nearest edge; collapsing a
-- flood to a point would drop it for users inside the flood but far from its
-- centre. latitude/longitude carry a representative centroid so the map, which
-- skips null-coordinate events, can render a marker.
--
-- Provenance (verified 2026-09-06, generated -- not hand-typed -- by
-- openspec/changes/fix-eonet-polygon-transposition/verify-transposition.mjs):

--   EONET_22248 -> GDACS 1104078 episode 1, 1311 vertices, country Cameroon
--     GDACS lon 9.2156..9.5688  lat 4.3770..4.8284   (EONET published these axes reversed)
--   EONET_23208 -> GDACS 1104105 episode 2, 28 vertices, country Nigeria
--     GDACS lon 9.8523..9.9306  lat 12.4681..12.5893   (EONET published these axes reversed)
--
-- ⚠️ GDACS publishes 4 decimal places where EONET publishes 6, so the restored
-- rings are accurate to ~11m rather than ~1m. Immaterial for a flood extent
-- spanning tens of kilometres, and stated so nobody later reads the reduced
-- precision as corruption.
--
-- ⚠️ 1104078 re-enriches to Cameroon, NOT Nigeria. Its true extent still
-- intersects the Nigeria ingestion bbox because that box overhangs the border,
-- so the row legitimately stays in the dataset. The trigger finds no Nigerian
-- ADM1 match and falls back to the Cameroon ADM0 outline loaded by 000012,
-- leaving state_name NULL. That is the designed behaviour for border spillover,
-- and the honest result: a Cameroonian flood labelled Cameroonian.
--
-- Targeted rather than generalised
-- --------------------------------
-- 000011 could generalise because any point outside every configured bbox is
-- provably a leak. No such predicate exists here: a transposed pair is still a
-- valid coordinate pair, and the correct value cannot be computed in SQL -- it
-- has to be fetched from GDACS and matched to the right episode.

BEGIN;

-- EONET_22248: GDACS 1104078 episode 1 (1311 vertices)
UPDATE events
